Overview {#mainpage}
========

WiiLand is an open-source user-space stack for Nintendo Wii and Wii U remotes
and compatible devices, including the Wii Balance Board, Wii U Pro Controller,
and extensions. It uses the official Linux `hid-wiimote` kernel driver through
the `libxwiimote` library.

The common input path is:

		hid-wiimote -> libxwiimote -> wiilandd -> Linux uinput/evdev
		                         -> Wayland compositor/libinput
		                         -> X.org input stack
		                         -> SDL, Steam, Wine/Proton, and native apps

`wiilandd` creates display-server-neutral virtual Linux input devices. The
same gamepad and desktop profiles work in a Wayland session and in an X.org
session; the daemon does not require XWayland and does not call a
display-server-specific input API. Consumers see ordinary evdev devices
through their normal input stack.

Virtual devices are named `WiiLand Virtual Controller` and `WiiLand Virtual
Desktop`. Older builds or application profiles may refer to
`WiiLand Wayland Controller` and `WiiLand Wayland Desktop`; the observable
rename is intentional. Applications that match input devices by name may need
to be remapped to the new names.

This distribution is hosted at:
  https://github.com/PhilosophiMoonbeam/wiiland
Use this website to contact the maintainers, developers, or issue tracker.

Build and install
=================

Build dependencies
-------------------

The normal build requires:

* a C compiler, a C++17-capable compiler, GNU make, and `pkg-config`;
* Autoconf/Automake and Libtool when generating a build from a checkout;
* `libudev` development headers and library;
* Linux headers providing `linux/uinput.h`.

`ncurses` development headers are optional. They enable the `xwiishow`
terminal frontend when `--enable-xwiishow` is enabled (the default is
automatic). Qt 6 Widgets development headers and libraries are optional.
They enable the `wiiland-config` control center with `--enable-qt-ui`; the
default is also automatic. Qt platform plugins are runtime components, not
build dependencies, and are not checked by `configure`.

Runtime and permission requirements
-----------------------------------

At runtime, load the distribution's `hid-wiimote` kernel module, provide the
`libudev` shared library, and run BlueZ for Bluetooth pairing. `wiilandd` needs
read access to the `hid-wiimote` `/dev/input/event*` nodes and write access to
`/dev/uinput`. Install the WiiLand udev rules or use the distribution's normal
logind, udev, or input/uinput-group policy. The daemon, not the Qt frontend,
opens these input devices and `/dev/uinput`.

If `wiiland-config` is installed, the Qt Wayland platform plugin must be
installed for a native Wayland session and Qt's `xcb` platform plugin must be
installed for an X.org session. The required plugin must be able to connect
to the active session display. WiiLand does not silently switch to another
plugin or retry with a different display backend.

Configure, build, and install
-----------------------------

From a release tarball, use the included `configure`. From a checkout where
it is absent, generate it with:

	$ ./autogen.sh [configure-options]

A complete distribution-style invocation is:

	$ ./configure \
		--prefix=/usr \
		--sysconfdir=/etc \
		--enable-xwiishow=auto \
		--enable-qt-ui=auto \
		--with-udev-rules-dir=auto \
		--with-systemd-user-unit-dir=auto \
		--with-xorg-conf-dir=auto
	$ make
	$ make install

`--enable-xwiishow=auto|yes|no` controls the optional ncurses frontend.
`--enable-qt-ui=auto|yes|no` controls the optional Qt 6 frontend; `yes`
turns a missing or unusable Qt 6 Widgets development setup into a configure
error. `--enable-debug` enables a debug build.

The integration-directory options each accept `auto`, `no`, or an absolute
custom path:

* `--with-udev-rules-dir=auto|no|PATH` installs the udev rules. `auto`
  uses the `udev` pkg-config directory, with `${prefix}/lib/udev/rules.d`
  as fallback.
* `--with-systemd-user-unit-dir=auto|no|PATH` installs the user unit.
  `auto` uses the systemd pkg-config directory, with
  `${prefix}/lib/systemd/user` as fallback.
* `--with-xorg-conf-dir=auto|no|PATH` installs the X.org `InputClass`
  policy. `auto` uses the X.org server sysconfig directory, with
  `${datadir}/X11/xorg.conf.d` as fallback.

`no` leaves that integration metadata out of the install. A custom `PATH`
must be absolute. These switches change installed integration files; they
do not change the display-neutral uinput backend.

Installed package contents
--------------------------

The package installs:

* `libxwiimote.so`, its public header, and `libxwiimote.pc`;
* `wiilandd`, the uinput daemon;
* `xwiishow` when the ncurses frontend is enabled;
* `wiiland-config` and its desktop entry/icon when the Qt frontend is enabled;
* `wiilandd-hardware-report`, an installed host and hardware diagnostic
  command;
* `wiilandd.conf` under `${sysconfdir}/wiiland` and as a documentation
  example;
* `70-udev-wiiland.rules` and `70-wiiland-uinput.rules` when udev
  installation is enabled;
* `wiilandd.service` in the configured systemd user-unit directory when user
  units are enabled;
* `50-xorg-fix-wiiland.conf` in the configured X.org directory when X.org
  policy installation is enabled; and
* the WiiLand, libxwiimote, daemon, and enabled-frontend manpages.

`xwiidump` is built as a non-installed diagnostic program. It reads Wii
Remote EEPROM data and requires kernel `debugfs` support.

Pair a remote
=============

Pair through the desktop's Bluetooth settings, or use the current
`bluetoothctl`. For the command-line method, make the remote discoverable
(for example with its sync or pairing buttons), then:

	$ bluetoothctl
	[bluetooth]# power on
	[bluetooth]# agent on
	[bluetooth]# default-agent
	[bluetooth]# scan on
	[bluetooth]# pair XX:XX:XX:XX:XX:XX
	[bluetooth]# trust XX:XX:XX:XX:XX:XX
	[bluetooth]# connect XX:XX:XX:XX:XX:XX
	[bluetooth]# scan off
	[bluetooth]# quit

Replace the address with the one reported by `scan on`; desktop Bluetooth
panels provide the equivalent discovery, pairing, and connection operations.
The obsolete `xwiibind` pairing workflow is not part of WiiLand.

Configure profiles
==================

The daemon reads `/etc/wiiland/wiilandd.conf` first, then
`$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/wiiland/wiilandd.conf`, or
`$HOME/.config/wiiland/wiilandd.conf` when `XDG_CONFIG_HOME` is unset.
Later values override earlier values. `--config PATH` selects one explicit
file instead of this hierarchy; `--no-config` uses built-in defaults.
`res/wiilandd.conf` is the installed sample and a useful starting point.

The default `gamepad` profile creates one `WiiLand Virtual Controller` per
remote for games and other evdev consumers. `desktop` additionally creates a
`WiiLand Virtual Desktop` mouse/keyboard device: D-pad repeat and valid IR
events move the pointer, A/B click, +/- send Enter/Escape, Home sends the
overview key, and 1/2 send Page Down/Page Up. `both` enables both devices.
These profiles and commands are identical on Wayland and X.org:

	$ wiilandd --list
	$ wiilandd --profile gamepad
	$ wiilandd --profile desktop
	$ wiilandd --profile both

Set persistent defaults in the user file, for example:

	$ mkdir -p "${XDG_CONFIG_HOME:-$HOME/.config}/wiiland"
	$ printf '%s\n' 'profile=both' 'pointer-speed=24' 'ir-speed=10' \
		> "${XDG_CONFIG_HOME:-$HOME/.config}/wiiland/wiilandd.conf"

The sample also documents IR tracking and optional screen calibration,
desktop button remapping, per-sysfs-path and per-device-type profile rules,
and motion aiming. `aim-mode=right-stick` exposes camera aim on
`ABS_RX/ABS_RY` for Steam, SDL, Wine/Proton, and native games;
`aim-mode=mouse` exposes relative aim through the virtual desktop pointer.
Use `wiilandd --device N --calibrate-aim` on a flat, still surface and copy
the emitted key/value lines into the same configuration file when calibration
is wanted.

X.org raw-device policy
=======================

When the X.org integration is installed, `50-xorg-fix-wiiland.conf` tells
X.org to ignore raw Wii Remote event nodes that would otherwise create a
duplicate pointer. Its InputClass exceptions retain the existing Classic and
Pro Controller behavior. This is X.org policy only: it does not block
`libxwiimote`, `wiilandd`, or either `WiiLand Virtual *` uinput device.
Wayland compositors do not read this file. If a packager uses
`--with-xorg-conf-dir=no`, install no X.org policy and configure equivalent
raw-device handling only if that X.org deployment needs it.

User service
============

When the systemd user unit is installed, it is a user service wanted by
`default.target`. It runs `wiilandd --check-config` as an `ExecCondition`
before starting, so an invalid configuration is reported without entering
the restart loop. Enable and start it in either display-server session:

	$ systemctl --user daemon-reload
	$ systemctl --user enable --now wiilandd.service
	$ systemctl --user status wiilandd.service

After editing configuration, restart the unit to apply it:

	$ systemctl --user restart wiilandd.service
	$ journalctl --user -u wiilandd.service

If systemd or the installed unit is unavailable, `wiiland-config` reports
that state and does not replace the service with a privately spawned daemon.

Qt control center and deterministic QPA checks
==============================================

When built with `--enable-qt-ui`, run:

	$ wiiland-config

The frontend uses Qt Widgets/QPA and contains no X11- or Wayland-specific
input API. It validates configuration before atomic save, launches daemon
diagnostics and traces, and controls the installed user
service with `systemctl --user`. The default user file is read by the next
service start, so the GUI can restart that unit to apply it. An explicitly
selected file is not loaded by the packaged unit; the GUI disables its restart
action and shows the exact `wiilandd --config PATH` command instead. The status
area reports the QPA backend selected by Qt.

For deterministic package checks, explicitly require each plugin rather than
assuming that environment detection or fallback proves availability:

	$ QT_QPA_PLATFORM=wayland wiiland-config
	$ QT_QPA_PLATFORM=xcb wiiland-config

The first command must start and connect through Qt's native Wayland plugin;
the second must start and connect through Qt's X11/xcb plugin. A missing
plugin or unusable display is an actual check failure. `configure` does not
test either runtime plugin.

Diagnostics and validation
==========================

The following checks do not require a connected remote unless noted:

	$ wiilandd --version
	$ wiilandd --help
	$ wiilandd --check-config
	$ wiilandd --dump-config
	$ wiilandd --doctor
	$ wiilandd --axis-map
	$ wiilandd --validation-checklist
	$ wiilandd --self-test

`--doctor` reports the detected Wayland/X11 session variables and sockets,
the resolved profile/backend, and `/dev/uinput` access. A Wayland socket is
not expected in an X.org-only session. `--check-config` validates without
enumerating devices; `--dump-config` shows the resolved layered values;
`--axis-map` and `--validation-checklist` print deterministic mapping and
hardware-coverage data. Help and list modes bypass configuration, so they
remain usable while investigating a broken config.

Use a dry run for event diagnostics without creating virtual devices:

	$ wiilandd --list
	$ wiilandd --dry-run --trace-events --verbose \
		--device N --profile both
	$ wiilandd --dry-run --trace-events=motion-plus --device N
	$ wiilandd --dry-run --trace-events=ir --device N

Replace `N` with a number from `--list` or a `/sys` device path. The installed
report gathers host/session, Bluetooth, kernel-module, version, config,
permission, device, axis-map, checklist, and doctor information:

	$ wiilandd-hardware-report
	$ wiilandd-hardware-report N
	$ wiilandd-hardware-report N --trace-events=motion-plus
	$ wiilandd-hardware-report N --trace-events=ir

With a device argument it captures a live dry-run trace; exercise the
hardware and stop with Ctrl-C. The report records diagnostics and manual
validation placeholders; it does not claim that a real-hardware matrix has
passed. Validate consumers separately in an SDL tester, Steam Input,
Wine/Proton, native Wayland/X.org applications, and XWayland applications
where those environments are part of a deployment.

Development
===========

See `./DEV` and the manpages for development and API details. The hardware
free daemon smoke script is `tests/wiilandd-smoke.sh`; the install smoke
script is `tests/wiilandd-install-smoke.sh`. For real hardware, use the
installed report above and preserve its output with the kernel, distribution,
Bluetooth adapter, device type, session type, and application results.

Documentation
=============

